    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 17",
    "text": "-11- others. Of necessity, emphasis on tb::.;·~ was delayed until other aspects of the College were strengthened. General support by the University was substantially in~reased , and sig- nificant financial help was provided by NEC and AID, both within and outside the Contract. Of particular importance was special legislation to place ·Makiling Park under the control of the College and to provide strong financial support for the extension and research programs of the College. These accoEpiishments resulted from efforts by the Contract staff and the .faculty to make nee.d s kno'W!l and to enlist support. It is the considered opinion of the members of the visiting Contract staff that, although progress was slower than desirable, it was as rapid as could be expected. Adequacy and Appropriateness of Means Adopted Guidelines for judging the adequacy and appropriateness of means adopted -......_ in carrying out a progra;m of this type are difficult to define and difficult to apply. If the means adopted create confusion, opposition, or antagonism in the host institution , they are not effective no matter . how wise and appropriate they appear to be otherwise. On this basis the means used in the College program can be ·assessed as being appropriate and effective. Support by the administration and faculty of the College of Forestry was excellent, and the relationships of the visiting faculty to the resident faculty were, almost without exception, the best possible. The objectives of the visiting professors were almost always well understood by the resident faculty and were mutuaily supported and defended by both. The difficulties encountered were those inherent in the situation and not in an~ important degree involved between the personnel of the visiting group and the resident faculty. There existed a climate of respect and cooperation that may be considered as perhaps /",
