    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 29",
    "text": "- 23- two semesters. To correct this deficiency, and also to upgrade the programs generally, plans have been made by both the College of Agriculture and the College of Forestry to shift from four to five-year curricula in all fields of agriculture and forestry, beginning in the first semester of 1965- 66. The new five -year forestry curricula are being prepared so that they will strengthen education in basic sciences and humanities as well as in professional subject- matter fields . It is expected that superior students graduating from the five -year curricula will be adequately prepared to pursue postgraduate education in the United States. Objective 1 , therefore, has been largely attained and will be further strengthened when the five-year curricula go into effect . It should be added, however, that no progressive university expects curricula revision ever to be completed. Curricula are subject to constant study, review, and revision, and this can be expected to be the case also with the University of the Philippines College of Forestry. Obiective 2 . Training faculty members. The withdrawal of Bureau of Forestry support in 1957 left but seven senior faculty members including a dean; a secretary; a fiscal officer; two non- foresters teaching English, Spanish and Botany; and two full-time forestry pro- fessors. They were SuPplemented by six young instructors in March of 1957. Of the 13 m~mbers , only four held advanced degree s. This small group was attempting to provide instruction for some 400 students. Expansion of the fa- culty was imperative. A careful review of foresters active in the profession in the Philippines revealed that they fell into two major age classes: senior men who had re- ceived graduate education in the United States or other countries and who held important positions in their country, and young men who had graduated only a few years before. or the older group, most were appr~ching the age at which",
