    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 31",
    "text": "-25- retirement was required at the College of Forestry. The undergraduate education of the second group was seriously inadequate for the responsibilities of college teaching. Consequently, there was no choise other than to recruit the academi- cally superior graduates from among recent classes , and send as many as possible abroad for postgraduate education. This has been done . The faculty numbered 49 (Table I) at the close of the contract , 32 of whom had been sent abroad for additional education and training, 29 under the ./ SUNY contract (T abl e II). Three former faculty members were trained and r e - ceived degrees abroad also, but have since left the College . Advanced d~ gre es have been awarded 22 of the current faculty, one of whom has attained the Doctor of Philosophy degree. In additi on, one has earned the Master's degree at the Universi ty of the Philippines Diliman campus, so that 45 percent of the faculty now have advanced degrees. Seven faculty members have received non- degree training abroad. In the summer of 1965 ten faculty members were out of the Philippines on advanced s t udies. Nine of these were in the United States, and one was in Germany. Four of those in the U. S. were working on Ph.D. programs. Seven other faculty members were scheduled to go to the U. s. in the fall of 1965 fo r fo rmal advanced e~ucation, five as AID/~EC sponsored participants, and t wo under independently arranged assistance programs. Two additional faculty are working on Masters degrees at the U.P. College . of Agriculture for the maximum of six units per semester allowed resident faculty. Nine of these 19 had formerly received the Masters' degree and are, or will be, working toward the Doctor of Philosophy degree. If the current advanced training program is successful, 67 per cent of the current faculty will hold advanced degrees. Obvi ously, it is not possible for a faculty of 49 to handle 500 students with over one-third of its members outside the country taking graduate work without considerable sacrifice both t o t he individuals and to the College",
