    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 43",
    "text": "-37- not released until ten months of the fiscal year had gone by. This fund should · amount to nearly 500 ,000 pesos annually, hereafter . Measured against the re - sources available to it up to this year the research activities of the faculty have been. commendable . Measured against the immense amount of r esearch needed , however i only ·a bar~ beginning has been made . The t raining and experience of the faculty i s improving to a point wher e significant research can be carr i ed on as soon as the teaching load is distr i buted among more peopl e . With t he improved faci l ities and more ample .f unds now available, however, more research could be done under existing teaching l oads. Leader s in Philippine f orestry recognized that illicit or unregulated logging , squatting and kaingin making-on p ublic_ lands were rapidly r educing the luxurious forests of the country to unproduct ive cogon grass and brushlands . Without public support , the Bureau of Forestry was virtually hel pless t o s t op the denudat ion. With the leadership of the visiting technicians, a General Forestry Committee was organized in earl y 1961 comprised of the heads of the various government fo r estry agencies and a representative of wood- using in- dustry . Just prior to this action, a Joint Committee on Public Information and Education in Forestry had been formed consisting of public information personnel from each of the cooperating agencies . It was later agreed that the public information program would be centered at the College. The current chairman of the Department of Forestry Extension at the College, co~sequently, has served as chairman of the Joint Committee since its establishment in 1961 . I t was largely through the joint support of these committees and forest indus - t ry leaders that RA3523 was passed . College budget planning calls for alloca- tion of a little over half of the income from RA3523 to its forestry extension program.",
