    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 99, part 1",
    "text": "F 0 R E W0 R D This is the report of a special review team requested by Dean D. L . Umali to assist him and his colleagues to evaluate the needs for expanded programs in instruction, research and extension and for improvement in the physical facilities of the College of Agriculture, University of the Philip- pines. The purpose was to help prepare a plan which would greatly strengthen the College in its programs to serve the needs of the people through educa- tion, research and extension . The team of consultants represent the joint interest of the Ford and Rockefeller Foundations and Cornell University . All of these orga~izations have a long history of sincere interest and active participation in programs of International Agricultural Development. They have cooperated and worked closely with the University of the Philippines in the past and the recom- mendations resulting from this study provide opportunities for further col- laboration for the mutual benefit of the Philippines, the Southeast Asia region and the United States. Throughout this review the consultants have received full cooperation from Dean Umali, his Directors of Research, Instruction and Business and Administrative Affairs, and all members of his staff . This cooperation is greatly appreciated . The consultants commend all of the staff for their preliminary preparations and for their prompt response in supplying needed data and information. Without such fine cooperation this review would not have been possible. Also the consultants wish to express their thanks to Dr . R. F. Chandler, Jr ., Director of the International Rice Research Institute, and to Dr. Horst von Oppenfeld, Visiting Professor, Council on Economic and Cultural Affairs, for their counsel and advice. F'urther, we express our thanks to the Rockefeller and Ford Foundations and to Cornell University for giving us the opportunity to make this review . It is our hope that the recommendations may be implemented so that the u. P . College of Agriculture may continue to grow and develop into a more out - standing research, teaching, and extension resource for the development of the agricultural potentials of the Philippines and",
