    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 102, part 1",
    "text": "3 ~ (The remainder of the recommendations are listed within a s~ecific area of o~eration at the College . No attempt has been made to list them in order of importance . ) General and Organizational Affairs 6. It is urged that efforts be made to bring about greater flexibility of salary items that will ~ nnit promotion of the most competent staff throughout the College and more efficient use of availabl e salary funds . All salary items might well be considered in relation to the needs of the College as a whole and should not be \"frozen\" , within departmental or administrat ive budgets. 7. Continued efforts should be made to strengthen and give greater flexibility to t he offices of the Directors by: (a) setting up separate budget items f or the position of director independent of departmental budgets, and (b) expanding their duties and responsi- bilities to upgrade the qual ity of their res~ective programs. 8. Most of the assistant inst ructors, research assistants and lower grade instructors should gradually be replaced with half- time graduate assistants working in research and/or teaching. This should upgrade t he competence o:r the teaching and research staff and also greatly expand t he graduate education program. c::: 01 CD p, 9. Consideration should be given to the establishment of a new depart- ment of Agricultural Infonnat ion ~nd Connnunications to ~dite the dissemination of agricultural information and to provide instruction in the art and science of communication. This departm~nt probably should be housed in an enlarged College administration building. Instruct i onal Program 10. The pres ent course offerings of each de:partment should be care:rully evaluated periodical~ in terms of: (a) expected course content in relation to staff com~tency, (b) total number of courses, es~cially in relation to the size of student enrollment and to the subject matter organization, (c) the possibility of canbining similar c,ourses and the offering of some of the more s~ecialized courses in alternate years. 11. Ex:pansion of graduate studies shoUld take place in a given depart- ment no more ra~idly than the increase in the number of high quality staff in that and",
