    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 103, part 1",
    "text": "4 ResearCh Program 14. Vigorous attempts should be made to obtain a higher proportion of the College's research budget through the regular u. P. annual appropria- tions. This will permit the employment of permanent staff and the making of long- ter.m plans for research based on the needs of the country (see Figure 1). 15. Through the Office of the Director of Research, continued emphasis should be placed on research quality and output through: . (a) redue- ing the number, increasing the specificity, and improving the qual- ity of research projects, (b) allocation of research fUnds to depart- ments and specific projects with the concomitant expectation of speci- fic accomplishments, and ( c expansion and improvement of cooperation between departments and with other research agencies, such as the Bureaus of Plant Industry, Animal Industry, and Soil Conservation. Agricultural Extension 16. The activities of the College in agricultural extension should con- (.) tinue to expand, especially in the training area, through: (a) the ro p.. appointment of a Director of Extension whose duties and authority would be similar j.n his area to those of the Directors of Research R ro m and Instruction, (b) the appointment or employment of at least one extension specialist in each of the subject matter departments to c:::: m ro help plan and expedite an enlarged extension program and (c) the p.. provision of building space for the office of the Director of Ex- tension in an expanded administration building. 17. Some funds for agricultural extension should be allocated through the regular U. P. Budget to supplement the outs ide support for this work. The expanded extension program should be based on long-term objectives in agricultural de·velopnent of sufficient importance to the University of the Philipp::tnes and to the Philippine government to attract their participation. 18. The Farm and Home Developnent training program and other such educa- tional techniques which might be developed should be integrated as effectively as possible with the activities of other action agencies concerned with extension such as the Bureau of Agricultural Exten- sion (BAE), Presidential Assistant",
