    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 106, part 1",
    "text": "7 The Cornell proposal was reviewed in a conference at the College in February, 1962 involving Dean Umali and other staff members , former Dean W. I. Myers and Dr . Richard Bradfield of Cornell University. I nter ests were expressed in exploring further the possibilities f or continued coopera- tion between the U.P . College of Agriculture, Ford Foundation, Rockefeller Foundation, Cornell University, and the Philippine and United Stat es govern - ments. The importance and complexity of t his matter i ndicated that a team of consultants should be invited t o assist Dean Urnali in preparing a plan which, if implemented, would greatly strengthen the College in its efforts to serve the needs of the peopl e of the Philippines. Brief Hist ory of t he College and I ts Functions From a modest beginning in 1909, with four faculty members and·. twelve students , the U.P. College of Agriculture rapidly grew and developed in stature prio r t o World War II . An Experiment Station was established in 1914 but no f unds were appropriated for its operation until after. 1950. As a result, the early development of the station was achieved through ' savings from funds allocated primarily for educational purposes. From almost t otal destruction during World War II, the College emerged s t ronger t han ever with the help of U.S. war damage and technical assistance programs, t he Philippine government, the Rockefeller Foundation, the Council on Economic and Culutral Affairs, and Cornell University. The \"Cornell-Los Banos \" cont ract , under the auspices of ICA, was started in 1952, \"to assist in the r ehabilitation of the College of Agriculture, the development of the Cent ral Experiment Station, the training of professional agricultural per- sonnel in research, teaching and extension, and the development of educational materials.\" This contract was terminated in 1960. During the operation of this contract 51 specialists in all fields of agricultural sciences from American universities, 35 of whom were· from Cornell, participated and served as visiting professors and colleagues of the Filipino staff in teaching and research. Also, 83 faculty members of the U.P. College of",
