    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 109, part 1",
    "text": "10 Executive Secretary; and Mr. Richard Bernhardt, Chief Programming Officer, AID. Separate conferences were held earlier with Vice-President Pelaez and Mr. Carlos P. Romulo, the new President of the University of the Philippines. These discussions were most profitable to the consultants and should be very helpful in future plans of development for the College. Local representatives of the Rockefeller Foundation and the Council on Economic and Cultural Affairs were kept fully informed of the review team's activities. Their counsel and advice were enlisted in the evaluation of present programs. Further, all recommendations contained in this report have been discussed fully with them. Finally, all important aspects of the evaluation of present programs and facilities and the proposals for further development of the College as herein reported were developed jointly with Dean Umali, his directors, and members of the major College committees. There was general agreement on these recommendations with enthusiastic expressions of a desire for their implementation in order to increase the effectiveness of the College of Agriculture in its role of service to agriculture through education and research. In fact, steps have already been taken to implement some of the recommendations. Evaluation of Present Programs General and Organizational Affairs The reviewing team was very much impressed with several aspects of the College program which are important essentials to any successful College or University. First and foremost was the most cooperative attitude of all members of the academic and non-academic staff toward efforts to improve their College and its program. The Dean and his directors were especiall~ helpful in encouraging as complete a study of their College as possible and in jointly preparing the suggestions for improvement included in this report . Secondly, the team was delighted to find generally high morale among the academic staff as a whole. There are some real problems facing the faculty and the administration - problems to which solutions must be found. But these problems are being recognized . Furthermore, there appears to be a feeling of confidence on",
