    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 110, part 1",
    "text": "11 Directors of Instruction, Research, and Business Affairs . They agree whole- heartedly with plans to give greater responsibility and authority t o the offices of these Directors and with tentative plans to set up the office of Director of Extension. They are also enthusiastic in their support of the recent move to set up the position of Director of Research independent ot any department and to release the position vacated by him to his former department, Plant Pathology. This should set a precedent f or the other Directors, whose offices and responsibilities would then be i ndependent of their subject matter fields . The review team was impressed with t he advisory committees which had been appointed to work vrith the Directors and with the f act that the commit- tee membership is from each of the foQT main areas of the College: (1) physi- cal sciences, (2) plant sciences, (3) animal sciences, and (4) social sciences. These committees will help assure consideration of the needs and problems of all segments of the College. If a rotational system of term appointments or election to these committees were to be worked out, it would also help pro- vide continuity of policies and procedures . The Consultants agree with Dean Umali and his directors that the area of physical sciences and mat hematics needs st rengthening and that this might best be done if these uni ts wer e housed t ogether and perhaps administered by a division head. Thi s arrangement would undoubtedly improve the quality of instruction in the physical sciences. It would simult aneously allow agricul- tural engineering, the present home of physics , meteorology, mathematics, and statistics, to develop more fully as an essential unit of the College. The public r elations and public service activities of the College have been improved in recent years by a number of activities including field days, short courses, an expanded program of t he of fice of Extension and Publications , and cooperat ive activi t i es with many other agencies such as the Agricultural Cr edit and Cooperatives I nst i tute (ACCI). The experimental Farm and Home Developn.ent (FHD ) t raining projects have also shown how effec-",
