    "text": "18 maintenance of a quality staff'. This fact was thought to be the pr~ry rea- son ror the loss of professorial staff in chemistry and physics, areas wherein outside competition for well-trained scientists has been especially keen. Although there are some obvious disadvantages to salary differentials between subject matter fields, if the College is to maintain its current staff in all areas and is to recruit replacement personnel in the physical sciences, the competitive factor must be taken into consideration as one of the criteria for salaries and promotions. There is one administrative policy which in the past may have been a - factor in making salary increases difficult. This is the tendency for aca- demic positions to be \"frozen\" within the department where they are budgeted. This policy tends to prevent flexibility in use of available salary funds and may be an important factor in contributing toward personnel problems relating to inadequate salary increases. The review team is in complete agreement with the suggestions of Dean Umali that upward adjustments in staff salaries must be made. If the salaries- of the competent, productive young assistant and associate professors through- out the College cannot be increased, the loss of staff such as has occurred in chemistry might well spread to other departments and much of the gain made in staff competence would be l ost. Faculty Housing and Social Environment Inadequate low cost housing and associated utilities were rated by those staff members who were interv.iewed as the second most serious problem facing young faculty members. There was enthusiastic support for the steps taken by the College to provide building lots on College property and to arrange for long-term loans for the construction of homes. A speed up in the expansion of this program would do much to make L:>s Ba.Iios a more attrac- tive living area for the faculty and would probably be a factor in holding some staff-who have opportunities elsewhere. (See Table 5 for summary of current housing needs.) The housing needs of unmarried academic personnel were also em]hasized. These young peopl~ make up a high proportion of the total staff and",
