    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 119, part 1",
    "text": "20 tenance of its physical facilities and equipnent. Next to faculty salaries, the review team feels that the maintenance problem is the most serious one facing the College administration and one that will be a continuing deter• rent to the progress of the College. A problem for any educational insti- tution, it is particularlY critical for the College because of several fac- t ors: (1) the initial inadequacy of the basic utility units such as the telephone and electrical system for the expanded physical plant (for example, the telephone switchboard was designed for 60 sets but is used currently for more than 200 telephon~); (2) inadequate funds in the budget for the main- tenance of the physical plant; (3) no budgetary provisions specifically for equipnent replacement; ( 4) a hot humid climate that plays havoc with many · scientific instruments; . and (5) inadequate local shop facilities for equip- ment maintenance and slow equipment repair and replacement procedures. Perhaps the most disturbing factor relative to maintenance is the in- ability of those supporting the College to have included in the bu4get adequate items for maintenance and for equipment replacement. New research budgets carry no items for plant maintenance nor is there any provision for \"overhead\" in a:ny of the grants to the College. BuQ.gets for research funds are generally top heavy with personnel and wage items with little, if any, provision for the upkeep of an expanding physical plant and equipment· in- ventory. As a consequence, the administration is faced with a most unhappy choice - the physical plant and equipment must deteriorate or funds requested for other purposes must be used for maintenance. Casual observation would indicate that a compromise has been the result - attempts at maintenance be~ yond that feasible with the limited budget allocated for this purpose:- -·Even this effort has been inadequate to maintain satisfactorilY the condition of buildings and equipment. The review team is in complete agreement with the College administra- tion9s request for a more realistic maintenance budget and inclusion of a specific allocation in each year's budget for equipment replacement.",
