    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 358",
    "text": "t 1 - 2- will be suggested is that we have a simple and direct tool to compare com- munities - rural and urban, cross-cultur al and across tlllle - on the general dimension of community differentiation . Differentiation is defined as \"the degree to which the community projects diverse areas of meaning . rrY Areas of meaning are reflected by the diversity of institutions, division of labor and frames of reference ._g/ The c6nununity then is seen as a storage for socially me aningful information. By Guttman scales analysis, vre will tap the degree of differentiation or the level at which the connnunity is capable of processing information. There are many ways to view community, and as many ways proposed to measure developme nt . k community .can be seen as an ecological unit , a place of interaction or a geographically defined social system. Depending on the orient at i on, developm3nt can be measured in a variety of ways . Develop!I13nt of a community can be measured in terms of per capita income , literacy rate , mean educational attairument, number of toilets built or the proportion of popula- tion placing a value on getting to places on time . The view taken here however , regards the community from a different vantage point . As suggested previously, the connnunity is viewed as an inform- ation processing organism. Its capability of handling and processing inform- a measure of its ability to survive or develop . Differentiation is a of storage and handling inforrration, and is reflected in diverse areas meaning . The social structure is seen as an information pool and tha hand- of this information is revealed by the presence or absence of publicly cognized areas of me aning such as the cumulation of netwo:rKs and diversity fral'OOs of reference . A- network refers to a pattern of publicly dis criminated relations including specialization of labor, marketing relations, and of social interaction.",
