    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 540",
    "text": "- 75 - large number of fertilize r expe rime nts throughout the Philippines. Th se exper ilnents are als o being conducted on farm ers' fields. Their e xp erimental work is s till in the process of being analyzed and the data is not available. The e xperiments of IRRI and of the Philip- pine Sugar Institute are controlled agronomic experiments. Of the various agenciee mentioned above, the Philippine Sugar Institute has perhaps done the most work to date in the economic analysis of ferti- lizer response. Analysis of the Bureau of Soils records has provided some in- teresting preliminary results. Forty-six experiments (with 3 to 4 replications each) were examined in which nitrogen had been applied at the zero, 30, and 60 kilogram l~vel. In the shift from zero to 30 kilograms of nitrogen the return over cost for fertilizer use was on the average, 4 to 1. However on 20 per cent of the experiments, the 1 yield ir..crease was not sufficient to cover the cost of the fertilizer. In moving fron 30 to 60 kilograms of nitrogen the results are even more striking. The returns above cost was on the average, 2. 5 to 1 for this second 30 kg. of nitrogen. However approximately 60 per . I cent :.)f the ·situations did not show a profit. These results suggest that fertilizing rice, particularly with the commonly used rice va- rieties, can be a risky business. Preliminary results of this study were reported at a d3part- mental seminar on June 2, 1966.",
