    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 1004, part 1",
    "text": "- 30 - Agronomy Research Laboratory Committee . The ~isiting professor was appointed immediately after his arr~val as a member of the Agronomy Research Laboratory Committee and he was shown an empty, newly completed, air-conditioned room that was to. sez:re as the laboratory. The writer made a very small contr~but~on to the unpacking of equipment to be used in the laboratory ~d to getting the laboratory ready for operation. The laboratory ~s now extensively used by the staff members from the Horticulture Division who are working with post-harvest physiology of fruits and vegetables. r ~:;~~ • /t.f~ It ~s recommended that other Agronomy Research Laborator~~to obtai~ chemical and physioloRical data to complement their field exper~ments. Photosynthesis Field Instrument Set-up The writer visited the Photosynthesis Laboratory very soon after his arrival and was pleased that the personnel most involved with the laboratory requested his assistance. With help from members of the Photosynthesis Laboratory Committee and from interested Agro- nomy, Botany, and Research Farm Operation staff members, a review was undertaken. A report was submitted to the College Administration indicating the College is extremely fortunate to possess one of the best field photosynthesis laboratories in existence. Possession of this facility permits research in a field currently receiving more and more attention. It enables the College to supplement field research with investigations of the physiological basis for yield differences resulting from either variety or cultural practice differences. Such research is simultaneously both basic and applied to an extent that is possible for few research areas, and is currently of extreme interest to all agronomists. Significant research results have been obtained at the Photosynthesis Laboratory. The report emphasized that completion of research underway, publication of results, and maximum use of the laboratory could be best achieved if: (1) The laboratory was given a budgeted allocation for operation, and (2) A professor was assigned to the laboratory on a full time basis. Research Project on Photosynthesis and Yield Physiology The Visiting",
