    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 1006",
    "text": "- 32 - Coordinated National Vegetable Variety Evaluation A Droject for coordinated vegetable variety trials at sev:ral Bureau of Plant Industry Experiment Stations and several agr1cultura~ col~eges lo:ated throughout the Philippines is being planned. F1nanc1al back1ng is being sought for such a program. Develo ment of Very few persons have been assigned to work full time as vegetable extension agents or technicians with responsibility to extend vegetable production information directly to the farmer. To the extent that such information is extended, it is accomplished either by technicians of the Agricultural Productivity Commission and other agencies whose technicians have broad assignments, and t~erefore, spend most of their time with rice and corn, or by d1rect contact between the farmer and personnel of the College of Agriculture or Bureau of Plant Industry. The working section of the Society for the Advancement of the Vegetable Industry (SAVI) that is concerned with extension has declared that there is great need for a body of extension workers with specific assignments to extend veget- able production information. Recommendation Every possible effort should be made to: (1) Create a corps of extension workers with specific assignments to work full time at exten- ding vegetable production information to farmers. (2) Once such persons have been assigned to work on vegetable crops they should be trained in vegetable production at the College for a minimum of three months. Training of Vegetable Extension Personnel During the last several months of the 1967-68 fiscal year, a national drive was begun for increased vegetable production in the Philippines. In a program sponsored by the Rice and Corn Pro~uction Coordinating Council and the Bureau o~ ~lant Indus:ry, approx1mately 560 persons were given a one-week tra1n1ng co~rse :n vegeta~l: produc- tion. The College of Agriculture cooperated 1n th:s by tra1n1ng 26 persons to be trainers of additional grou~s of :ra1nees. The Coll7g7 also trained a total of 60 additional tra1nees 1n ~ro one-week tra1n1ng sessions, and a group of 25 air-force personnel for a two-week period.",
