    "text": "- 45 - This inve~t~gation shows that ~his particular species varies greatly in its ab1l1ty to attack certa1n susceptible hosts s uch as tobacco peanuts, ginger, and pepper. The isolates used also vary consider;bly in their appearance on various types of culture media and ability to utilize certain compounds in their growth. A survey of the distribution and economic importance of Pseudomonas soZanaaeapum in the country has been completed. Results shows that this pathogen is a serious problem in every major agricultural region of the Philippines except parts of the Mountain Provinces, and severely affects tomatoes, potatoes, peppers, eggplants, ginger, and tobacco. Abaca, peanuts, and a few other crops are less severely affec- ted. It is estimated that bacterial wilt causes 13,000,000 t o 15,000,000 loss in agricultural production in the country each year. Prof. R.G. Davide and the writer investigated a disease which results in the hardening of fruits of the cooking banana in the Dumaguete City area. This disease is causing heavy losses in parts of Negros and ~1indanao , and is known as tapUPok in Negros and Bugtok in Mindanao. Experimental evidence indicates that the disease is caused by a bacterium, as yet unidentified. Extension Extension activities for this year included the presentation of a 15-minute radio talk on the research being done on bacterial wilt and a two-hour talk on vegetable diseases and their control, which was presented to agricultural representatives of the Agricultural Develop- ment Council for Rizal.",
