    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 1149",
    "text": "- 27 - PHYSICAL SCIENCES AND NATURA.L RESOURCES (3) Thomas Wickhatll Graduate Assistant in Agricultural Engineering Introduction This participant in the U.P.-Cornell Graduate Education Program has been in the Philippines conducting research for his Ph.D. dissertation for almost exactly one year. During that time the facilities of the Department of Agricultural Engineering have been made available to him on a generous basis. A good start has been made in the research and a meaningful place found in the U.P.C.A. community. Teaching No teaching was included in last year's activities. However, a graduate level course in Water Management (Agricultural Engineering 243) is now being taught to about nine students, for this first term of the 1969-1970 school year. Its focus is proper management of irrigation systems, rather than their design, for optimum performance. Effects of various management levels on crop production, ways in which high management levels dictate system design, and institutional factors affecting management will be explored. Research Objectives The purposes of this participant's research problem are: To determine the water requirements of rice as it is 1. actually grown in important areas of Luzon island To determine the contribution of irrigation in meeting 2. these requirements, given the rainfall regimes encountered in the areas",
