    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 1209",
    "text": "INTRODUCTION Established in 1909, the UP College of Agriculture grew slowly but steadily until it was almost completely destroyed during World War II. After the war, it painfully emerged from the rubble. However.. development was slow until the Cornell- Los Banos training program financed by an ICA- NEC\"~ contract brought help. Under this program, operated from 1952-1960, fifty one professors from Cornell and other institutions in the United States came to Los Banos to help their Filipino counterparts develop strong undergraduate teaching programs in various phases of agriculture. UPCA was on its way to becoming the best college of agriculture, not only in t he Philippines but also in all of Southeast Asia. In the early 1960's it became evident that a str ong graduate education program was needed to complement the good undergraduate foundation which had been developed at UPCA. This, in turn, required a strong research program for graduate education cannot be separated from research. Good research scientists attract graduate students. But good research requires adequate physical facilities, and the buildings and laboratories at the College were crowded and inadequate. Thus, in :196{, the College initiated a five-year development program. With considerable help from a loan from the World Bank and from matching funds allocated by the Republic of the Philippines, UPCA embarked upon a $12 million building program. This program is more than 90 per cent complete, and many new and functional buildings have risen on the campus. The UP-Cornell (UPCO) Graduate Education Program covered by the present report was funded by a grant from the Ford Foundation. P art of this grant went to Cornell University and part to UPCA. The major objective of the UPCO program is to develop and strengthen graduate instruction and research at UPCA. To accomplish this, emphasis has been placed on faculty training and staff development, library acquisitions, equipment for instruction and research, and financial support for research projects. International Cooperative Agency of the United States (the predecessor of AID) and National Economic Council of the Philippines.",
