    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 1226",
    "text": "Adolfo Necesito (agronomy - Held crops) has completed a study of soil-plant water relationships for tobacco, and is presently summanzing tis <!<lta. He hopes to complete his thes1s and receive his Ph. D. degree m June 1971. William Fernandez (plant pathology - microbiology) is working on his thesis research which involves a study of the various micro- orgamsms that occur in immature and mature coconuts in the Philippines. Fellowship Grants for Non-Academic Personnel The College has grown tremendously durmg the past decade. It has expanded its activities and assumed multifaceted roles of leadership in agriculture. While staff development was accelerated to strengthen UPCA 's capabilities in meeting its responsibilities, the supporting services have lagged behind. As a solution, non- academic fellowship grants were conceived under the UPCO Program to improve the training and competence of administrative personnel m key areas and thus increase their capacity for service in the academe. These small fellowship grants involving payment of tuition and college fees and cost of transportation have been given to selected non-academic personnel since 1966. Under this fellowship program, 15 non-academic personnel have completed studies toward the bachelor's degree at nearby colleges. Of these 15, three earned the AB degree, eight, the BSC, two, the MPA and two, the BSE (see Appendix B). Two of the grantees graduated~ laude. Five current fellowship grantees are yet to complete their studies under the UPCO program. Two of these are taking M.S. in library science, one is taking the MPA degree and two department clerks are working for the BSC and BSE. Short-term, Non-degree Training Grants The degree training program for non-academic personnel was discontinued during FY 1969-70. Incumbent grantees were allowed to continue with their studies, but no new fellowships were awarded last year. Attention was focused on the need for short-time technical training of personnel in such areas as electronic equipment maintenance, general me.::hanics, diesel engme maintenance, refrigeration repair, and scientific 11lustratwn and photography. 21",
