    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 1857, part 1",
    "text": "REPORT CN AGHiaJL'lURAL EDUCATIQil STUDIES TO THE UNIVERSITY GHADUATE SCHOOL STANDING COMr-IITTEE aJ EDUCATI<N S'IUDIES Pr epared by the Department of Agricultural Education , u. P. College of Agriculture A. Degree Programs .5ill9. Instruction The Department of Agricultural Education off ers an M.S .. degree \"lith majors in the fields of Agricultural Education, Agricultural ExtensiQn Community Development, Rurnl Sociology, and a Ph.•D. program in the first two fields . Am::mg the features of the graduate progrc.m in this Department are: 1. Advanced training in the above-mentioned fields are geared toward the enhancement of professional competencies for teaching, res earch, and action . Regardless of what the student expects to do after completion of his gr aduat e degree, a thesis b e~.sed on an empirical study is required,. · The intent is not to make every· student a r es ear cher but to expose teachers, administrators, extension and C. D. workers alike to the r es earch process as an avenue for recognizing r es earch as a tool for action~ administration, t eaching, etc. and as a w~y of imbibing into the student the ethos of the scientific approach to problem- solving. There is no question, however , that several of the graduates take up r esearch as a major part of their career ,. 2. At least a year of experi enc e related to their chosen major field is required , in addition to the usual graduate school r equi r ement s f or admission . In the case of community development and rural sociology majors , at least 18 units of social science courses at the undergraduate level are required ,. 3. Faculty members engaged in graduate teaching and thesis advising are mainly concerned with th e graduate progr am . Their responsibilities with undergraduate students are minimal or none at all hence their time and energy are devoted to the needs of graduate students. 4. The staff of the Department of Agricultural Education is v ery int erdisciplinary and hence minimizes th e possibility of falling i nto a narrow academic groove as a means of professional self- defense . A cro s s- fertilization of ideas is inevit able because the graduate progr am has o een developed and",
