    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 1894",
    "text": "THE EFFECTIVE PRESENTATION OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH FmDINGS FOR USE BY STUDEN~9 m TBE AGRICULTURAL HIGH SCHOOLS A Cooperative Project of the Bureau of Vocational Education and the U. P. College of Agriculture During the past ten years extensive researches have r~sulted in drasti e revisions in the recommendations made to farmers for the production, processing, marketing, and utilization of the va- rious agricultural enterprises . Massive efforts have been made by both governmental and private agencies to communicate these findings to farmers . The success of these efforts is to be seen in the acceleration of changes in farming methods throughout the country. There is a need to extend the pipeline, through which agri- eultural research findings flow, to students in the agricultural high schools . Not only is there a scarcity of references avail- arle for their use but too often what they have is either un- suitable or out-of-da te~ Many of the students in the agricultu- ral nigh schools will be ' the farmers of tomorrow. They need the results of scientific research to improve their farming programs; to make them aware of -the relationships between research, im- proved farming methods, and success; and to assist them in learning to solve farming problems through the intelligent weigh- ing o~ alternatives. Experience has shown that student learning is improved when there is ready access to up-to-date instructional materials which are: based on research findings, written at appropriate reading and interest levels, presented from the farmers point of view; appropriately illustrated, and set forth in correct language. The goal of this project will be to make such materials readily avail- able to the students in the agricultural high schools throughout the country. 1. To determine the reading level of students in the National Agricultural Schools. 2. To ascertain the enterprise areas in which student manuals are most needed in the National Agricultural Sohools. 3. To obtain suggestions for improving the student en- terprise manuals previously developed and distributed by the College of Agrioulture.",
