    "text": "3 programs, teeching methods, fecilities, te~cher trAining, etc. The second study tour wes deliber~tely structured to build on end extend the initiPl effort by: sendino; three stP.ff members not involved in the first tour , including South Koree emong the countries to be visited, end by supplementing the written meterial resulting from the first tour with case study reports of strong local programs in the four Asi?n countries visited . Purposes 1. To observe and describe strong innovative programs of voc- ational egriculture pt the secondary school level in various Asian settings . 2 . To confirm and extend professional relationships with Agri- cultural Education l eaders in Asian countries. Procedures Letters of i nc:uiry \\-rere sent on February 27 , 1969 to the direct ors of Agricultur al Education programs in Thailc.nd , Republic of China, Japan, and South Korea informing them of tentative plans for the study tour and reauesting information concerning: innovc~ti ve and outstanding local progrems, te acher training institutions, and contact persons with whom advance tour arrangements might be made in each country. As a result of these inoulrles official invitations were received from the directors to visit specific innovative and outstand- ing loc al programs , te 2cher training institutions, and ministries of education in all four countries. In each case a central office staff member was designated to coordinate details of the visit and to make internal travel arrangements. In order to systematize the collection of data, an interview schedule was developed, field tested, and revised. The schedule in its final form provided for the collection of detailed case study information concerning such aspects of a local school progrem as: loc~tion, history, main agricultural enterprises in the area served by the school, trends in agriculture in the area, edministration of the school, financing, purposes, curricula, main buildings, descrip- tion of site, water source, teaching staff, admission requirements, student fees, enrollment , drop-outs, evaluation, methods of teaching, student organizations, land, crops, animals, equipment, practice requirement, and adult",
