    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2028",
    "text": ".. 7- a rop. The other half is spent in classroom work to discuss the theori s of rice production technology. To integrate the various field operations in such a short time 1 arrangements vtere made so that rice plants at various stages of growth and land at various stages of readiness were available simultaneously at the training center. The preparations include the following: 1. land ready for plowing1 2. seed ready for pre-sowing treatments and sowing, 3. rice seedlings ready for transplanting, 4.. land already plowed, ready for basal fertilizer applica- tion and transplanting, 5. crops at flowering and heading stages, where diseases and insects can be studied and treated. 6. crop ready for harvesting, and 7. harvested crop ready for drying, cleaning, and weighing. B\" Principles of Learning Applied: To make the learning situation more meaningful to the trainees and make their training relevant to their work, tile two basic principles of (1) learning by doing and (2) teaching by showing were applied. Any training activity, be it in the classroom or in the field is designed to accomplish change-in behavior (the way the trainees do certain things, say something, think and feel). The progr.am had been organized to provide appropriate activities which were challenging to learners. The trainees",
    "href": "Cornell (1).txt"
