    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2088, part 1",
    "text": "- 2 - re n t entirely enoo gir~. The index of manufacturing production 1 sh s an incre se of 12.5% per year sino_ 1948. The gross national product incrc sed 84 per cent between 1948 and 1958. An important part of these g ins was the result of postwar recovery and the domestic produotipn of goods that were formerly imported. The continued ind atrial growth of the Philippines will have to depend more and more on tne expansion of the domestic market. Since 1956 the growth of the gross national product has just kept up with the population increase. Due to the added effect of inflation, there has been no increase in real wages since 1956. The price index rose 22 percent be~veen 1955 and 1960. The employment of total population has increased from 35 percent in 1948 to 53 percent in 1958 but without the desired increase in productivity per worker. The present population is 28 million and estimates show that the rate of increase is rising and will reach J.2 by 1965. The crude birth rate of 49 is the highest in the Far East. The death rate has fallen to some extent as a result of the control of malaria and a marked decline in tuberculosis. Forty-six percent of the population is under 15 years old and there are 89 child dependents per 100 people in the age group 15-64. This is said to be the highest ratio of child dependents in the vforld. The rapid increase in populati on creates serious economic problems although the potential for food production has not been reached. The population per hectare of cultivated land is 4, about half that of Taiwan and one-fourth that of Japan. In a surve.y of households published in 1957 the average family income was reported to be 1,471 pesos, equivalent to $370 at the present free rate of exchange. The average family income in rural areas was 989 pesos, about one-quarter that of Manila and half that of other urban areas. The average expenditure for food was 46 percent of income. Twenty percent of the families had 50 percent of the total income. The Economic Research Service of the USDA has made a long-term projection of supply and demand for selected agricultural products in the Philippines. This shows almost a three-fold increase in",
