    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2181",
    "text": "FRUIT HYSIO OGY, CHEMICAL AND PHYSICAL PROPER A WATER RELATIONS I. INTRODUCTIC:tT 1. Fruit defined: From the botanical standpoint, a fruit consists of those tissues which supports the ovules of a plant, and whose development is depen- dent upon physiological changes occurring in these ovules (Nitsch, 1952, 1953). Fruits may also be defined according to their lo~ation within a dietetic dimension and in accord with popular experience . \"There is no well-drawn distinction between vegetables and fruits in tha popular sense; but it has been held by the courts that all those which like po- tatoes, cabbage, carrots, peas etc., are eaten (whether r aw or coo!:ecl) with the principal part of the meal are to be regarded as v eg\"et ables; while those used only for desserts are fruits\" Webster's International Unabridged i: ictionary. Hughes (196 ?. ) also states \" Those food commonly c~;:;signated as fruits are pulpy in character, often juicy, and since they develop from flowers of plants, t hey consist of the ripened seed or seeds vri.th some edible tissue attached\". 2. Vegetable defined An edible herbaceous plant with some portions eaten raw or for culinary purposes. They are also commonly consumed with salt Ll'l contrast to horticultural frT:o its whicl:. cs.ce genera:!.ly (;Onsumed with sugar. They may either be ~nnual or perennial grown as an annual. II. METHODS GF CLASSIFYING FRUITS .?\\.ND VEGETABLES 1.· Botanical or taxonomic basis-e. g. angiospern1s~ monocots~ dicots etc. 2. Based on temperature requirement- e. g. temperate~ tropical, subtropical etc. 3. Based on the length of life cycle- e. g. annuals, biennials and perennials.",
