    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2184",
    "text": "A list of the tropical fruits of importance are shown in Table 1. Fruits assum e various forms and sizes ranging from the digito- form contours of the banana to sphe rical shap~ of the guava. The forms of tropical f r uits do not fit the geometry of classical pomology. Figure I show som e of the more com1non morphological fruit types. The anatomy of fruits is interesting as an aid in identifying the origin of the pulp. Other points for study are the presence of stone cells; the micrograins of starch; the microcrystals present in some fruits, such as pineapple and pomegranate; and other elemen::s of structure. For example the mH~rograins of starch in the unripe mango are different from the micrograins of other plant prcducts. For de- tails of fruit anatorny of different fruit types such as the £apodilla, pomegranate and breadfruit see Lindorf (1966) for banana, Vvinton and Winton ( 195 8) and for anatomy of some tropical fruits see Griebel (1928). Vveir and Stocking (1950) have studied the histological changes in fruits and vegetables during proce s:::;ing, particularly by heat. Because of their organoleptic and chemical properties, fruits are eaten in the natural or the processed state; before meals to stimulate the apetite, or to slake the thirst; or as dessert after the main course, to cleanse the palate of the flavors of soup, fish or meat. 4",
