    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2187, part 1",
    "text": "Compared with some plant products, fruits are of relatively low caloric value. They do, nevertheless, contribut e to human nutrition through- out the world. Fruits supply abundant vita1pins, mineral salts, and carbohydrates. The aron1atic and gust atory substances, together with sugars, orga::.: -~ acids, and tannic substances, intensify secretion. A relatively ric.~. cellular structure and the pectic substances contribute to the dynamics of digestion . Fruits are also used for ·medicinal purposes. Finally, fruits are utilized fo.c their flavor and beauty. They are t:sed as spices and adorments or table preparations and artful displays as well as ceremonial symbols in v a rious social and religious feasts. V. FRUIT DEVELOPMENT The tissues of the fruit do not arise de novo when the fruit is \"set\". They have developed along with thos e of theflower bud before the flower opened. Thus the life history of a fruit may include the following phases: 1. A pre- anthesis phase in which growth occurs mainly by cell division. · 2. A critical phase at anthesis during which pollination and sub - sequent fertilization of the ovules or the l a ck of these proce::;ses will determine the future development of the fruit . 3. A period of post-fertilization growth, mostly by cell enlarge- r..flent (cell multiplicaiio~ in the seed). 4. A period of maturation which in some fruits terminate with the onset of senescence. The fruit while attached to the plant is dependent on assimilatory processes including photosynthesis in leaves; on the capacity of roots to absorb minerals and waiP.r ~ and on the r aie of conduction of rnaterials through the vascular systern. Gro·wt h regulators moving into and out of .fruits also modify t heir growth and metabolism but the .r1ature of these various growth regulators have not all been characterized nor their mechanism of actiqn fully understood. Once the fruit is detached from the plant, t he processes of bio- logical oxidation with its oxi~ative and fermentative features, assumes a dominant role. The respirato~y tr£nd exhibited by floral organs, fruits and detached leaves are shown in Figure II. Note the sudden upsurge in respiration during a certa",
