    "text": "STORAGE TEMPERATURE Refrigerated storage of produce retards ripening, softening, changes in texture and color, reapiration, loss of moisture, wilting, spoilage, and undesirable growth, such as sprouting of onions and potatoes. For best results , tem perature should be held fairly constant at values indicated in tables 3 for fruits and in table 4 for v e getables. Fluctuations in temperature tend t o caus~ Inoisture condensations on stored products. This favors the growth of fun gi and bacteria result- ing in mold infection and soft Pot . Variations in temperarure can be prevented if storage rooms are sufficiently insulated, hav':; adequate refrigeration equipn1.ent, and if the diffe r en9 e between the temperature of the refr igerating coils and the ternperature of the storage room is small. RELATIVE HUMIDITY With only a fe-w exceptions (for example, onions and garlic), the most desirable condition is that in which the humidity of the air in the storage room is kept at approximately the moisture conter..t of the p~oduct stored inside. If the air is dry, the product will J.ikqly wilt or shrivel. If the air is too moist, they may decay. Leafy vegetables need between 90 and 95 per cent relative humidity; root cropr.: and fruits ) from 85 to 90 per cent. STORAGE LIFE Maximum storage life can be obtained only by storing products that are free from decay, bruises, sunscald, skin breaks, and other forms cf deterioration. The data on storage life given in tables 3 and 4 ar__~ · t approxnna e va lueC' ...... Thes \" be influenced by the source, - o-- mav · dmatu11ty, ult, 1 and variety of the product handling practices, climate, soll an c ura conditions in which the crops were grown. 34",
