    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2271",
    "text": "9. Sugars- sucros , corn syrup, candy, chocolat • 10. Beverages- coff , tea, juices, carbonated beverages, beer, wines. 11. L gum s and oilseeds- dry beans, mungo, soybeans, lentils. 12. Miscellan ous- spic s, salt, MSG. ote: crossovers-eg. butter is dairy & fat, juice is beverage & fruit, potato chip is vegetable +fat) hy this great variety in foods we eat? 1. Enjoyment- people like to have a wide range of flavors, textures and colors. 2. Nutrition-· no one group can supply all nutritional requirements . nutrition comes last again. ) y do we Process Food? To protect it from other living things that want tc eat our food a) animals- goats, dogs, rats, mice, birds. b) insects - worms, beetles, flies, mites, spiders. c) microbes - bacteria, molds, yeasts. B. To prevent losses in quality a) biochemical changes-. enzymes . b) chemical changes- .oxidation, staling, browning . . c) physical changes- desiccation, phase Heparation. To produce desirable changes in nature and quality eg-brea d from wheat. Prevent wastage at times of surplus and keep it until times of shortage. Nature provides feast or famine. Mankind has always been preoccupied with saving enough food from times of feast to carry over into times of famine. Joseph's,.., fat years and 7 lean years. Thanks to .modern food processing mankind has a bette r solution to this ·problem today than ever before in the history of the world. /--,\\ I I I') 7 ( I I ~ rJ '--··--·-- ,__.. ........· ..... , ... --'- / o l v' • (3)",
