    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2273",
    "text": "P rishable- last s veral w ks or months without spoiling- potatoes, apples, citrus fruits , fats & oils . Less pressing need for preservation. Stable. last months or years without spoiling- grains, nuts, oilseeds, honey. No preservation probler..:1 apart from adequat e moisture level. These foods feat ure strongly in the diet because of ease of keeping. Main storage problems are a) animals (keep out by fences or containers) and b) insects-st ore in insect-proof conta ine r s, maintain good sani- tation, fumigate if necessary. Irra diation s ometimes works. Use- ful r eference A. E. Michel Bacher \" Insects Attacking Stored products\" Adv. in Food Res i (195 3) 281 .. 8-4 8 • All foods deteriorate in s t orage \"In attempt ing t o preserve fo ods we are 1 __ , ....,... in the face of s ome of nature s most zeal ousl y enforced laws\" . Generally fo ods a r e a t thei r best at t ime of harvest, anrl then deterio rate . processing slows t he r at e of dete rioration, it doe s not prevent deteri oration. r Microbilogical spoi:!.age is usually rapid (within hours or days) . Biochemical spoilage is usually medium fast (weeks or 1noi1ths) Chemical spoilage is usually s low ( months to years). Methods Available for Pre s erving Food Method Length of time used 1. Keep it alive 104 years 2. Drying 104 \" 3. Sm oking and salting 10 4 II 4. Fermentation (EtOH, lactic acid, 104 tl acetic acid) 5. Heat preservation (canning) 160 (Appert 1812) 6. Chemical (a) food-sugars, food acids 100 years (b) non food-benzoate, so2 sorbate ( 5)",
