    "text": "42 EFFECT OF SOAKING SOYBEANS IN NaOH SOLUTIONS ON FLAVOR AND NUTRITIONAL VALUE In an effort to improve the flavor and acceptability of soy milk, several studies were conducted with NaOH additions during the soaking step. The initial study involved the preparation of soy milk samples which had final pH's of 6.55, 7.20, and 8.41. I These samples were submitted to the 14 members of the soybean research group for evaluation. All members of the group preferred I the most alkaline sample. Samples of soy milk were also prepared at five different pH values ranging from 6.56-8.85. These were I heat processed at 250°F for 10 minutes to determine the effect of I processing on acceptability. The milks were found to hold up quite well under process, the most marked change being a slight darkening I of the color. Two samples were also prepared for amino acid and Kjeldahl I nitrogen analysis. Both samples were prepared using a preliminary I soaking treatment of 1-1/2 hours at 50°C, followed by thorough rinsing and a hot extraction with distilled water. One sample was I soaked on .085 N NaOH (pH 12.7), and the other was soaked in distilled water. The resulting milks had pH's of 7.86 and 6.56, I respectively. Both samples of milk were found to contain 7.06% Kjeldahl nitrogen. Amino acid analysis of the two samples indi- cated that the two samples were very similar in amino acid compo- sition. However, cystine and tryptophan were not measured in this study.",
