    "text": "~ebruary 4, 1967 TO: Joint Commission on ~ural Heconstruction F: OM : Ramon v. Valmayor , In Charge of Hor t iculture College of Agriculture, University of the Phi l ippine s 3UBJBCT : Consultation on Horticulture I find agriculture in Ta iwan more advanc ed than \"the present stage or agriculture in the Philippines . I ha ve learne d much more than I can e ver cont ribute t o i mpr ove your agri cul t ure . Howe ver , I have three sugges tions tha t may help you in some l itt l e wS¥. 1) Adopt shi el d budding for c it rus in stead of g rafting . 5eedlings can be transplan ted to nursery some six months after sowing. I n another six mon t hs, they are ready ror budding . As in your g rafting meth od, t he bud dlings maybe all owed a year's growth he rore t r ansplanting t o the orchard . At transplanting ' ime, the huddl i ngs are two Years old . Your present practice takes three yYears ear. . The adop tion of s hi e ld budding will save your farmers one 2) Bud-union should he hi gh . -Mos t or your scion varieties or citrus are susceptible to root-rot . The rootstock variety used may be resistant to t his di s ease but with the present practice or grarting within 6 i nches rrom t he ground, the susceptible sci on , var i ety is too close to the soil where the rungi causing foot~rot l i ves. Every time it rains, the causal organism is splashed up t o the s usceptible ecion trunk porti on. Avoid this inrection by bud di ng high - 30 t o 40 em . At this height, the susce ptible sci on is believed to be beyond reach of fungus carried in splashes caused by rain drops . 3 ) Use only oxcortis-free scions for Rangpur lime r ootstocks . There seems to be a . decided switch from Sunki to Rangpur lime r oot- stocks . Ranipur lime is tolerant to tristeza but very susceptible to excortis vines. Make sure that the selected mo ther trees or sources of bud- wo od are free of excortis before budding them'to Rangpur lime rootstocks. The indexing method is very simple ~nd common knowledge. 'Your citriculturists know this. It was a pleasure working with you. Ramon V . Valmayor Ass't Professor in Horticulture",
