    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2487",
    "text": "4 Packing was slow. Some girls wrapped very slowly, the good wrappers should teach the new girls how to gently toss an orange into the ~Trapper in the other hand, the pressure will cause the corners of the wrap to come up so that one motion of the other hand will finish the wrapping. Could \"piece work\" be used to speed up the operation? Some girls were taking a long time to pack one box. Sizing oranges: Mac~ne sizing with a divergent roller could be locally made. Yet if labor is cheap enough it might not pay for itself. Hand sizing is still done on many apples because it results in less bruising. The girls hold the sizing rings with one hand; so~e girls passed every orange through a sizing ring; this takes time. Consider having the girls stand up at a bench, the sizing rings could be fastened to wood about one foot or less above the baskets being sized; the oranges should be placed not dropped. Consider putting the sized oranges into the boxes that will later be packed, the baskets are too deep and too heavy. I estimate that a completely hand operation could be made two times as fast as the present operation for sizing and packing. The wrapping operation would be faster if the girls had a \"needle dispenser\" for the wraps, these are always used in the U.S. when wrapping fruits. The girls sometimes fumble with several wraps at once, the dis- penser gives them one wrap at a time. / - wraps i ~-__./ Citrus Handling Procedures Remember that mechanization is not necessarily better. it must be cheaper and better than hand operations. The present trend in the U.S.A. is handling the fruit~ in water as much as possible because it is economical and more gentle. Any possible change should be studied on a small scale using time - motion studies as well as care in handling. A. Possible improvement in band operation 1. Girls stand a table or bench on which fruit rests tor siziDg",
