    "text": "THE DEPARTMENT OF HUMANITIES Brief History Before it acquired its present name~ the Department of Humanities had functioned under two nameso As the Department of English from 1919 to the outbreak of the second world war~ it was renamed the Department of Languages to escape the close scrutiny of the Occupation forceso It bore this name until 1963 when the staff voted to adopt the present name to indicate the Departmentvs additional fields of instructiono Ironically, the name that was given to the office to avoid offending the Japanese was the most appropriate for it at the time. Although English language and literature had been the traditional fields of the Department since 1909, German and French were also offered whenever English teachers and members of technical departments who had facility in either language or in both were availableo During the Japanese occupation when the teaching of English was forbidden, the Department offered German and Spanish insteado After the war two more courses -- Scientific Reporting and Public Speaking -- were added to the Department offeringso In 1961, with the introduction of the General Education curriculum, the offerings increased by about fifty percento From 1909 to 1950, the instructors in English and those in Spanish and German were guest teachers coming from the Department of Agricultural Education, Plant Pathology, and Soils, and the College of Forestryo In 1951, however, the first bona fide member",
