    "text": "~ · EVIEW OF THE UPCA -CORNELL PROGRAM I N CnADUb TE EDUCATION AND RESEARCH AT LOS BANCS By ltl.s Chandrastitya, Amando M. Dalisay, and J. Norman Efferson* May, 1967 The purpose of this review as anticipated in the Ford Foundation's grants to the UP College of Agriculture and to Cornell in 1965 was to eva- luate the progress of the assistance program from 1962 to date and to make recommendations as to desirable changes, if any, in the program. Background and Scope of Work A consulting team of three members, one each from the Philippines, the United States, and another Asian country was selected to assist the College, Cornell, and the Ford Foundation in making this evaluation. As a matter of convenience, the team members were employed as consultants of the Ford Foundation. The team members, however, had no special responsibility to the Ford Foundation by virtue of this fact, or to Cornell University or the University of the Philippines College of Agriculture, and the o p inions expressed herein may or may not reflect the thinking of one or more of these institutions. The team was requested to make its report formally to President Carlos F. Romulo of the University of the Philippines. It was also re- qtES1ed to make the report a comprehensive one in that the report would, in so far as possible, incorporate the views of the Dean of the UP College, the Dean of the New York State College of Agriculture and the Director of International Agricultural Development at Cornell, and the Represen- tative of the Ford Foundation in the Philippines. However, there was no requirement that full agreement be reached between the team on the one hand and the other parties, individually or collectively. The team attempted to reach such agreement on major issues through numerous conferences with all parties but where differences occurred which were not clearly in agreement with one or more of the listed parties, the opinions herein represent the observations of the consultant team. * Former Rector, Kasetsart University, Bangkok, Thailand; Consultant to the Rice and Corn Production Coordinating Council and to the Rice and Corn Administration, and An~res Soriano Professor of Economics,",
