    "text": "POST REPORT - THE PHILIPPINES Manila and Environs, Quezon City and Baguio Climate The weather in Manila and Quezon City is very hot and humid most of the year. The warmest months are usually March, April and M~y, when there is little or no rain and the daytime temperature ranges from 90°- 9 5° F. and the humidity 90 percent or more. The typhoon or rainy season usually follows and lasts until September, although in some years heavy winds and rains have occurred up to December. The coolest months are December, January and February, when the daytime temperature ranges betwee n 75°- 85° F. Nights are somewhat cooler. In Baguio, which is located in the mountains north of Manila, temperatures run twenty degrees or so cooler than Manila. Clothing (1) Women. It is recommend e d to bring a plentiful supply of warm-weather clothing from the States, mostly cottons (and blends of dacron and cotton) for day and evening wear. Some linens are worn, too. Dressy cottons, silks, chiffons and laces are very appropriate for cocktail and dinner wear. Shoes can be made to order at fairly reasonable prices, but it is advisable to bring a good supply from the States. Most women find cotton lingeri e more comfortable than nylon. A limited supply of American-made clothing and shoes is available but at very high prices. Dressmakers are many, but it is some times a trial to get a well-fitted dr. .ess. Hand- or machine-embroidered dresses of cotton, dacron (or tetoron) and cotton blends, ramie (linen-like fabric) and ramie-tetoram blends, jusi or pifia (very thin, dressy, natural-color fabrics made from the banana and pineapple plants) can b e made to order at prices ranging from approximately US$10 up, including the cost of the material. A few evening gowns would be useful in Manila, as well as a lightweight stole or two for wearing in air-conditioned restaurants or hotels. Swim suits should be brought as w e ll {several if you plan to do a lot of swimming, since they tend to wear out rath e r quickly.} White tennis clothes and shoes are worn on club courts. Hats are rarely worn, though you might like to bring a veil-type one or two, for church and weddings; gloves are worn on these",
