    "text": "- 6 - V-- Eyeglass pr scriptions should,brought, and these may b cost. Money and Banking The present exchange rate, which remains quite stable, is f>3. 89. to US$1, 00 ($3. 80 in hotels). Thus one peso is worth about 26 cents. Dollars may b exchanged into pesos quite re adily by drawing a check on a U. S. bank, and guarantee by a Foundation foreign staff member. There are branches of the First National City Bank, Bank of America a nd British banks in Manila, at which peso checking accounts may be opened and drawn on immediately with the deposit of a dollar or pound sterling check or cash. Shopping Department stores, beauty salons, barber shops, well-stocked drugstores, book and stationery shops are found in various area s of Manila, Makati and Quezon City. Se rvices are very inexpensive by American standards, but imported items, such as cosmetics, are high. Restaurants and Hotels There are a fair number of good restaurants and several adequate hotels in :Nianila, with a Hilton and a Sheraton recently completed and an Intercontinental pr esently under construction. (There is an endless number of night clubs, not all reputable,and a number have very good dance bands.) Transportation Most transportation is by automobile, and there are a number of fair roads in and around Manila and Quezon City (fine ones in Baguio), though traffic is very congested much of the time due to road repairs underway and the increasing number of vehicles (including the famed jeepneys, taxis and lumbering old buses) in the cities. The railroads are not adequate but the air service in the Philippines is excellent. For the trip from Makati to Diliman (where the University of the Philippines is located) and from Manila to Diliman, usually half an hour is allott-ed outside of the commuting hours. Churches The Philippines is a predominantly Catholic country, and there are many churches. A few Protestant denominations are found, and there is a synagogue in Manila.",
