    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2694",
    "text": "to some of our other allies throughout the world; that the United States aid large or small, which has gone to these allies might hav~ been responsible for keeping them on our s'ide' in the struggle between Communism and the free world; and that, but for that aid, the free world ':ould have been so weak that the Communists would have nsked a world war. Why, therefore, should we begrudge our other allies now the aid which t hey are receiving from the United States? Then, also, there is a limit to what an under-developed economy, like ours, can absorb in devel- opmental funds over a short period of time. The absence of well-conceived projects has, I understand, been respon- sible for the failure by us to make use even of the funds already allocated. And, finally, why should we complain about the size of a gift? The Philippines, as I have said, i~ a young country. We lack capital. We lack technical know-bow. It is a short- sighted policy indeed for us to reject American economic aid and technical assistance, or to debunk both, when there is so much to be done in order to develop our economy, st rengthen our democratic institutions, and make the bless- ings of the democratic way of life reach the masses of our people in the remotest rural areas. It is entirely proper, even while we receive economic aid and technical assistance from the United States, to be vigilant and to guard against the impairment of our sovereign rights, where these are invaded or threatened; but, let us not raise doubts about the motives of our benefactors or malign them unless there are valid reasons therefor; and there are ~one. In the world today, the Philippines has no better friend than the United States. Let there be more understanding and less suspicion of her motives. Let there be above all a little more gratitude. ' ' I e e • • • e . e e .· . ! I 10",
