    "text": "I I United States of America Operations Hission ( to the Philippines Manila TO: Staff FROM: Information Division TWo top writers of the New York Times have been in th e Philippines recently and have written their impres s ions of the country a nd President 1v~gsaysay . Following is the secorrl of their articles: By ROBERT ALDEN Nanila Regime Hqld Neither Success Nor Failure Aft er 15 Nonths in Office NANILA, March 14--Aft er fift een months in office, the administ ration of; President Ramon }~agsaysay is judged by observers here as neither a success nor a failur e . Howev er, the months ahead are regarded as critical. Des pite hard work by Mr . Hagsaysay , no significant improvement in condi- t ions her e has been not ed. There is still unemployment. There is still great dispar ity betweE_3n the wealth of the large lando1rmers and the almost feudal peonage of the bulk of the farm population, who serve the absentee landlords as tenant fa r mer s . Although there has been some i mprovement , there is still corruption. The r e is still injustice . The Goverrunent is still unable to collect income and r eal estate taxes efficiently . I . But the supporters of President Hagsaysay say that the roots of large- scale economic and social reform are in the ground and will bear fruit after a time . Even the President ts critics admit that:, in view of the obstacles in his path, it could hardly be expected that the present administration could haYe achi eved spectacular success during its short time in office. A Number of Difficulties The difficulties that beset ·Mr . Magsaysay, as summed up by his advisers and United States obs ervers here, follow: Trouble with an uncooperative Congress, made up in large measure of persons with vested interests. Reform bills that the President h9S urged on Congr ess are delayed by haggling and then are either shelved when the Congres sional session expires or are passed in an impractical form. -",
