    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2737",
    "text": "-2- The Philippines is only an infant republic--eight years old . United S t~tes observ rs feel that a new retublic is bound to stumble from time to ime . The problems in the Phili pines have been tough , there h~ve been short- comings here in handling them and at least in part , a ccording to Filipinos , there ~ave been sr.ortcomin£S of the United States in preparing them for democracy . Health Conditions !~proved The Unit ed St1tes made gr eat advances in improving health condi tions in the country and in expanding educational facilities . The United States , of course also laid the groundwork for democracy : training Filipinos t o t ake over the management of their country, teaching them the inst i tutions of democra cy, teaching them of their right to speak freely and write f r eelY. At t e same time the United States did nothing to br eak down the feudal system of landowning that was inherited when the count ry was t a ken over by the United States from Spain just before the turn of the century. Even when elections took ~lace here before the Philipnines was proclaimed free , the large landowners had themselves elected t o the l egi slat ure by their tenants, and there they were able to perpetuate the ir power. They are still being elected t o the l egi s l ature , and now the Goverrumnt i.S having difficulty instituting the ereatlY needed economic and social reforms that the United States failed to bring about when ~t governed th e countr,y. But the feeling of the common ueople he re toward the United Stntes is still most warm and friendly . Today as then it i s poss ible for an American to wan:ier anywhere through these islands and be r e cei ved in a fri endly, open-handed fashion by the Filipinos . Although the Co~unists would have it otherwise, the Filipinos and Americans still get along very well t ogether.",
