    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2743, part 1",
    "text": "- 3 - our successful private industry to join with the people of free Asia in building their private industry and facilitate the way . We cro1 consult and advise on the means by which a free nation builds upon the initiative of independent farmers to achieve a steady advance toward better standards of living, in contrast to the mounting failures of collectivist agriculture . It is clear that most of the nations of free Asia prefer to quicken their cooperative march toward these objectives th r ough the Colombo Plan Consultative Group which was established in 1950 to promote mutual economic development . We welcome this initiative . As a member of the Group , we shall continue to vm rk in strengthening its cooperative efforts . The v aried nature of national situations requi'res that our cooperation be essential ly bilateral . Some of the nations are memb ers of the Manila Pact and their treaty ob li gat i ons give rise to special economic problems . Mo st are membe rs of the Colombo Plan . Most , exc ept for Japan , have very little industrial capacity . The requested authorization includes substantial funds to f urther ou r mutua l obj~ctives in this area . Of t he se funds I suggest that we can achieve the maximum return if $ 200 million is set aside for the establishment of a President 1 s Fund for Asian Economic Development , with broad rul es enacted by Congress for ·its use through loans and grants , and with adequate latitude to meet changing circumstances and to take advantage of constructive opportun ities. To help assure ~he mo st effec tive use of these funds, this appropriation should be available for use over a period of years. Wisdom and economy in their use cannot be achieved through speed. A small, finn, annual commitment out of this $200 million may prove in many instances to be the most fruitful method . Becaus e of the continuing threat of aggression and subversion in Asia, a large part of the amounts requested for military assistance and direct forces support is to build and maintain the defensive forces of our allies there . This includes the substantial costs of maintaining and improving the defenses of the Nationalist Gover.nment of China",
