    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2778, part 1",
    "text": "- 9 - The r e sult should r each the farmers as quickly as possible so he could put them to use. In this difficult task, we have been l eaning heavily on the bxtension personnel. Incident a l ly, nearly all their ranking men, like those in Plant I ndustry, are graduates of the College of Agricul- tur e . Has too much emphasis been placed in this country on agricultural development at the expense of the industrial1 The fact is obvious that a healthy economy demands that our agricultural program be balanced with a certain degree of industrialization . Industrial research must, therefore , be properly attende d to . Unfortunately, the tendenc y in too many countries, including those in our part of Asia , such as Thailand, Pakistan, India, Formosa, and even our Philip- pines has been to launch a disproportionately ambitious progr~m of industrialization and shove agriculture to the backseat. A swing to this extreme ma y well spell disaster, as was unfortunately the sad experience in Argentina, in Peron's time, where the country's economy was nearly ruined through overindustrialization . Before we can industrialize to any considerable extent, agri culture , which is ' the basis of our economy, must first be strengthened. Vigorous, sus- taine d, and adequately supported research is necessary to solve the m~y important problems that still remain to be studie d and make our farms give more bountiful yield. For a good man~ crops superior seeds that could produce fourfold or more still await development . Better farm management has to be worke d out to make ope ration more economical and more e fficient, fertilizer nee ds have to be more pre cisely determined, numerous pests, disease s, and we ~ ds, that exact a heavy toll on the harvest , cry for more effective control. As it is now, the average Phili ppine farmer, it is estimat ed, can raise only enough for himself and a t hird other person besides . Compare that, for instance, with the American farmer, who besides feeding himse lf has enough left over for fourteen other persons. As we raise the efficiency of our agricultural worker, so much manpower could ~he n be released to the in~ustries without harmfully affe ct1ng",
