    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2840",
    "text": "/ GENERAL DISCUSSION of the e Plaoe of Rioe in the Agrioulture of the Far East. When the of- fioers of the Rookefeller Foundation first beoame interested in the agriculture of the Far East, attention was focused primarily upon the rice crop because of its daninant position in the agricultur e of the entire area. In the present survey, r ice continued to r eceive more attention than any other single crop, but an ef fort was made t o get same idea of the potentialities f or t he production of other bas- ic food crops. Rice will probably continue to be the outstanding crop in the area because of its unique adaptation to the monsoon season charac- teristic of most of the area. Most of it is grOlVIl on the broad al- luvial plains and deltas of the huge rivers. The soils are often very heavy and drain slowly. The annual rainfall of 60-100 inches is con- fined largely to t he monsoon season which commonly starts in June and lasts through Sept ember. During this period, the average rainfall ranges from 8 to 20 inches per month. Fanners have learned through centuries of experience t hat rice is one of the few food crops which can be suc- cessfully grown under such excessively wet conditions. It seems highly improbable that the present dense populations in these areas could have been possible without rice. Farmers have developed slowly thr ough the centuries types of rice suited to the wide range of local environments . In most areas, until fairly recently, the cultural",
