    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2841",
    "text": "2. practices used were those which the individual farme r could carry out \\v.ith his own labor and tha t of a few of hi s neighbor s . His only source of power was his muscles and those of his family, suppl ement ed at times by those of the famil y ox or water buffalo. His t ools wer e designed and made at home or in t he local blacksmit h shop . I n t he course of time, three diff er ent types of r i ce culture have evolved: 1. Rain- fed i rrigation. While t he total rainfall during the monsoon pe r iod is on the ~verage quite a~e quate to grow rice, the dis- t r ibution is often i rre gular and .undependable . Farmers have l e arned to reduce these hazards by s toring the water falling during the heavy rainfall period on the rice paddy itself. Two operations are usual- ly involved : 1. A low earth dyke - usually only 18-30 inches high is thr own up, usuall y on the contour, around a small area in the field which is either f airl y l evel or which can be made level. This dyke or \"bund\" holds back t he normal runoff and enables the farmer to con- trol t he surplus wat er by either holding it where it fell, or by con- ducting it to other paddi es at lower levels. 2. On most soils, con- siderable water will be lost by percolation into the subsoil. To reduce such l osses, the soil is usually made less penneable by ttpud- dling n t he surface soil by plowing, harrowing and trampling it while it is cover ed with 2-4 inches of water. This operation also cleans the paddy of weeds and leaves it in good condition for transplanting the rice seedli ngs. Most of the rice in the Philippines, Thailand, Burma, India, Pakistan, Indonesia and Indochina is grovm under rain- fed i r rigation.",
