    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2842",
    "text": "2 Contr olled Irr igation. While the practices described above reduce climatic hazards , t hey do not give s ufficient control over t he ater supply to ensure high yiel ds in seasons in which the rainfall is poorly distributed. This has led to the development of sys t ems of irrigation in which the farmer can control the amount of water on his rice fields at all t~ne s . If the r ainfall is deficie nt , he has a reserve supply which can be used t o s uppl ement the natural rain- fall . If the r ainfall is unusuall y he avy, he can safely- drain off the excess with the as s urance thqt i f the r ains should s uddenly cease , he can supply t he deficiency f rom his reserve supp~y . The water for this supplemental i rrigation is usually drawn from either (1), wells , (2) small local surface ponds or tames which are cons t ructed to store surplus runoff f rom local streams , or (3 ) large lakes or re- . servoirs formed by constructing dams or barrages across some of t he l arger rivers . These latter structures are of course ver y expensive t o construct , but ar e oft en che apest in the end, particul a rly wh en t hey can serve seve r al purposes, such as flood contr ol , el e ctr ical power development, and ci t y and village wat e r s uppli es as well as for ir- rigation . Such lar ge multiple purpose projects require large amounts of capital and are usually r estr icted to projects financed by govern- ments. About 95%of the rice gr mvn in J apan, and much of t hat in Java, is under cont rolled irrigation . Higher yields of rice are usually ob- tai ned when controlled irrigation i s practiced. Since other methods for improving the yield of r ice do not give maximum returns unless the water supply is contr ol led, it is probably sound policy far gov- ernme nts to give high prior ity to t he development of their water re- s ources .",
