    "text": ";. ll, 1964 THE COLlEGE NIDI/SLE.'TTER Page .3 The July 14 issue of the Newsletter featured a case study which enumerated ~ personnel leave College . This time, the same DAIC group, headed by Dr. Gloria D. Feliciano and st~fers H. A. Ables, R. N. Salcedo, C. M. Mercado and V, PB. Samonte, pre sent s \"why they stay on\" with a tip of the hat to the 17 interviewees in \" ••• admiration and gratitude for their unqualified support in this project.\" Some of the (!lestions answered in this case study are as follot'ls : Why do they stay? Is it for sentimental reasons though salary offered out- side is twice that of what he is receiving now? Is it the climate? Is it because of legal and/or moral obligations? Are they just waiting for more lucrative offers? Or are they staying to find out i f the 5- year development program could really be implemented? The interviewees are faculty members, 1.4 of whom are Ph. D. 1 s and 3 11. S. 1 s . All are high- ranking and hold key positions in their respec~ive fields . From a population of thirty-five, 17 were chosen, six from the plant sciences, four from the animal sciences, six from the social sciences and one from the physical sciences. The study of the f}ndings showed that the respondents stay in spite of offers from other university units; government institutions; private colleges, agencies and corporations; foreign universities; and inter- national organizations. Some reasons for rejecting the job offers are: the 5-year development program; support and encouragement by administration; excellent policies and good facilities of the College; graduate fellowships abroad and travel privileges; salary increases, housing and other facilities ~rejected by the development program; loyalty to the College; s ecurity in tenure; privileges such as retirement and free tuition of children; academic freedom; contacts with other professionals with progressive ideas; challenging research/teaching jobs; less political pressures; more opportunities to contribute to national agricultural development; sat1s- faction in work; good place for the family; opportunities for pro~essional advancement and growth; and excellent facilities for educating",
