    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2916",
    "text": "NEW YO ST T COLLEGE OF AGRICULTURE 400 ROBERTS HALL ITHACA, NEW YORK TEL. ARS -3158 F r Immediate Use Ithaca 1 N.Y.J June -A Philippine educator says that achieve- ments of a former project between his country and Cornell Univer- sity have led to the possibilities of a \"new, higher-level partner- ship for progress. 11 Speaking to the faculty of the New York State College of Agri - culture, Dr. D.L. Umali, Vice-President for Agricul tural and Forestry Affairs and Dean of Agriculture at the University of the Philippines , emphasized that if a new pro j e ct materializes, it could serve as a model of inter- univer sity cooperation in various parts of the world. The proposed project would str engt hen the graduate program of the University of the Philippine's College of Agriculture which trains agricultural leaders , techni cians , educators, and researchers for that country and others in Southeast As ia. This College at Los Banos and Cornell carried out an agricultural development program from 1952 to 1960 t o help rebuild the institution ravaged by World War II and to strengthen the agriculture of the Philippines. \"We salute the Co llege of Agriculture at Cornell for accepting the challenge to deve lop agr i culture internationally,\" Dean Umali said. \"This great inst i tut i on deserves the commendation of the developing nations f or its pioneering work and current leadership in international agricul tur al development. It has the ability to carry out its international missions in any setting and at any time . \" (more) 291-60 62863 Cooperative Extens!on Service, Ne\"':' York State Colleges of Agriculture and Home Economics at Cornell University and the United States Department of Agnculture cooperating. In furtherance of Acts of Congress May 8, June 30, 1914. A. A. Johnson, Director of Extension,",
    "href": "Cornell (1).txt"
