    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2926, part 1",
    "text": "-2- Sci nee has unravelled the secrets of nat ure a nd h s o e new vistas of hope f or t he up lif t of humanity. Th ugh science man has been ab l e to conquer space , break the sound barrier, and translate int o solid real i ty \\·that our orebears regarded as i mpossible . Indeed, no phase human endeavor has escap ed the trans forming touch of modern technology . In \"sharing their abundance to create more abundance \" in underdeveloped and deve l oping nations, there could not be a more appropriate and real istic approach by the de - veloped countries than to help their human resources for social and economic a dvancement . Education is the hand- maiden of progress . For mer President Merrill of Minnesota once said, \"Human welfa re depends upon science and tech- nology and democrat i c freedom . Without the first, man becomes a s l ave of na ture . vlithout the second , man be- comes the slave of his fellow men . Both are not possible without education. 11 Educating the youth is the safeguard to an enlightened government and a prosperous economy. A univer sity contract with an educational institu- tion of a developing country to train and develop leaders who can intelligently and courageous ly guide their count~y­ men in nation building, is a sound and meaningful invest- ment, dollar for dollar, for the following reasons: 1. It has a strong moral appeal because it involves education of people. This is the type of assistance which can achieve progress in the minds and hearts of people. 2. It has the least likelihood of being misunder- stood (i . e . sup~orting an unpopular regime or a selected few.) 3. It cannot be misconstrued as political or economic domination by the aid-giving country. Developing countries that have just achieved their independence are keenly sensitive to political or economic interference. Many universities , particularly the land-grant in- s titutions in the United States, are effectively relating their experience and know-how to help accelerate the de- velopment of agriculture in developing countries. Cornell is an excellent example of one such University. We salute the New York State College of Agriculture at Cornell University for",
