    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2927, part 1",
    "text": "- 3- equally deserves to be congratulated for the vigor of i t s 1 adershi in nter tional deve l opment ; for i t a experience in nte r t ing teaching, research and extension activi t ies ; or it ab i lity in carrying out i ts i nterna t i onal mi ss i on n any setting and at any time; f or i t s a wareness that understanding other people and the i r wa ys of life and cultivating their good will and f r i ends hip i s a means of achi e ving oneness of humanity . Because Cornell has ha d cons ide r a ble experience in training foreign students on it s campus , because it has a supply of faculty with a wealth of exper ience i n the a r t of university assistance , because it has val uab l e connections and good publi c relations with l oca l and foreign foundations and agencies, and because when it works in a foreign country, its visiting pr ofes sors work not only with the top officials of the institution and the national government , but a l so wit h i t s village folks , its international programs have been more effective and have accomplished better results t han s imilar under- takings of ot her universities . The succes s of the Cornell-Los Banos contract ·i s a bright example of how the interpla y of all the afore- mentioned f a ctors have strengthened Cornell's inter- national development program. Mor e specifically, the Cornell pr ogram undertaken ou t s ide the boundaries of its sprawling campus has r ebuilt and strengthened the U.P . College of Agricul ture . The s eeds of our College of Agriculture were sown on J une 14, 1909 by a f a culty of four American teachers and a student body of t welve . Hard work and dedication resulted in a phenomenal growth that was blighted by -the second World War . Wi th one malicious stroke, the in- vaders reduced to ashes the 36 years' accumulation of plants , animals , buil dings , facilities, collections, manuscripts and records . It was left to the handful of experienced f aculty members who had survived the in- famous rape of Los Banos to carry on the work and the fine tradition of t he college. I n the tremendous task of starting all over a~ain we received mu ch support from the u. s. government (AID~, the",
