    "title": "Cornell (1).txt: OCR page 2932",
    "text": "- 8- trained he i s , he will still have to have a bacl<ground or the livelihood, customs and traditions of the local people to be really e ffe ctive, efficient and appreciated. He should understand that in his activities he \\d ll have to cope with barriers of nationalism p r e j udice, outmoded ways of life and the most formidable, the barrier of ignorance. 3. He should tTeat his local counterparts as his equal or co-worker or as partners in progress. H1s counterpart would appreciate shar~ ing his reports to the mother institution be- for e he submits them. He should establish, not only official·, but also personal relation- ships with his co-workers. 4. He should bear in mind that the local people know best their needs and problems. He should, therefore, also listen to the suggestions made by his counterpart. 5. Too long an assignment for a visiting professor in a foreign country may tend to weaken his professional competence. On the other hand, he may accomplish very l±ttle if given a very short assignment. A two-year assignment seems to be a happy middle ground. 6 . The best way for the local people to learn, develop, and mature is to let them do things themselves. They will make mistakes, but if they do not, they t~ll not learn more and better. The visiting professor should not make himself indispensable; instead, he should work himself out of the job. 7. He should live in the same community of the con- stituents of the host institution and send his children to the same school which the children of local people attend. The pointers that I have just mentioned cannot guarantee the success of a visiting professor's work at the host institution under all sets of conditions. Rather, they are intended as guides to facilitate orientation and activities in his place of assignment. There is no magic formula for his success. Only through patience, good Will understanding, and a sense of service can he hope to ' accomplish his mission.",
